{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Lambs in their first year.</b> And if you ask: according to the one who says we follow the years of the world [i.e., the calendar year], how can lambs be offered on Rosh HaShanah? If they were born before Rosh HaShanah, when Rosh HaShanah arrives they would be considered two years old, since one day in the year counts as a year, and we require specifically 'a lamb in its first year,' as we learned in tractate Parah: lambs in their first year and rams in their second, and all of them from day to day [i.e., counted from birth, not from the calendar]. One may answer that according to him [that opinion] we can find a case, such as their eighth day falling on Rosh HaShanah, and even though they were born before Rosh HaShanah, they are not considered at that point to be in their first year, since they were not yet eligible to be offered before Rosh HaShanah, as it is written: ומיום השמיני והלאה ירצה לקרבן (and from the eighth day onward it shall be accepted as an offering)."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:20:08.581218615Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}